join Gogo (Neutral) left
join Hope (Neutral) right
Gogo: I'll tell you a true one this time. Not the fireside kind.
Gogo: Your grandmother once walked four hours to bring me quinine when my second husband had fever. Nobody asked her to. She simply heard, and went.
Gogo: She got back at midnight, soaked through, and the first thing she said to me was not 'is he alive.' It was 'I brought too much — you'll have extra for next time.'
Gogo: That was her. Already thinking of the next emergency before this one was finished.
- That sounds exactly like something a Nkosi — or a person from this house — would do
	Hope: That sounds exactly like something a Nkosi — or a person from this house — would do.
	Gogo: Don't flatter yourself into her yet. You've a way to go. But you're closer than you were.
- I wish I remembered more of her
	Hope: I wish I remembered more of her.
	Gogo: You're building new memories of her every day. Through this village. Through what you're rebuilding at that homestead.
	Gogo: That's not the same as remembering. But it isn't nothing, either.
Gogo: She would be unbearably proud of you. And she would never once have said so directly.
Gogo: Some things, apparently, you do inherit.
set {GameState.gogo_grandmother_memory_shared} = true
[signal arg="hearts:gogo:1"]
